% Section 3: Experimental Setup
% Note: \section{} header is in main.tex

\subsection{Iterative Self-Training Pipeline}
\label{sec:pipeline}

We study diversity dynamics through an iterative self-training loop where each generation produces training data for the next. The base model is Qwen3-0.6B (596M parameters), chosen as the smallest model capable of coherent instruction-following while permitting complete multi-generation experiments in tractable compute budgets. We use UltraFeedback \cite{placeholder_ultrafeedback} as the prompt source, sampling 2{,}200 general instruction-following prompts (2{,}000 for training, 200 held out for evaluation). Each generation proceeds in four steps: (1) generate $K$ candidate responses per prompt from the current model; (2) score all candidates with ArmoRM-Llama3-8B-v0.1 \cite{placeholder_armorm}, a reward model that does not participate in training; (3) select the top-1 candidate per prompt according to ArmoRM scores; (4) fine-tune the base model on the selected subset to produce the next-generation model. We run three complete generations (Gen~0 $\rightarrow$ Gen~1 $\rightarrow$ Gen~2), where each generation comprises the full four-step cycle. Gen~0 metrics reflect the model after the first round of self-training, not the unmodified base model; the variation in Gen~0 Vendi Scores across conditions (105.42 to 108.08) confirms that even the first training round produces configuration-dependent diversity levels. All candidate generation uses temperature $T{=}0.7$ to maintain output diversity within each candidate set.

\subsection{Experimental Conditions}
\label{sec:conditions}

Table~\ref{tab:conditions} summarizes the seven configurations. Our design isolates four factors through controlled comparisons. First, adaptation method (LoRA vs.\ Full Fine-Tuning) isolates the effect of parameter capacity: LoRA \cite{placeholder_lora} updates only 1.67\% of parameters (rank 16), while Full-FT (Full Fine-Tuning) updates all parameters. Second, candidate pool size ($K{=}4$ vs.\ $K{=}8$) isolates selection pressure, corresponding to 25\% and 12.5\% retention rates respectively. Third, learning rate (5e-6 vs.\ 2e-5 for Full-FT) isolates learning intensity, spanning a conservative to aggressive optimization range. Fourth, curation strategy (ArmoRM top-1 vs.\ random selection) isolates the causal effect of reward-based filtering: configurations C5, C6, and C7 serve as matched baselines for C1, C3, and C4 respectively, differing only in whether selection is reward-guided or random.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Experimental configurations. Each curated condition (C1--C4) has a matched random baseline (C5--C7) that differs only in selection strategy. Trainable \% refers to the fraction of model parameters updated during fine-tuning.}
\label{tab:conditions}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lllcll@{}}
\toprule
ID & Adaptation & $K$ & LR & Curation & Trainable \\
\midrule
C1 & LoRA ($r{=}16$) & 4 & 2e-4 & ArmoRM top-1 & 1.67\% \\
C2 & LoRA ($r{=}16$) & 8 & 2e-4 & ArmoRM top-1 & 1.67\% \\
C3 & Full-FT & 4 & 5e-6 & ArmoRM top-1 & 100\% \\
C4 & Full-FT & 4 & 2e-5 & ArmoRM top-1 & 100\% \\
\midrule
C5 & LoRA ($r{=}16$) & 4 & 2e-4 & Random 1-of-$K$ & 1.67\% \\
C6 & Full-FT & 4 & 5e-6 & Random 1-of-$K$ & 100\% \\
C7 & Full-FT & 4 & 2e-5 & Random 1-of-$K$ & 100\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

This factorial structure enables direct attribution. If reward curation causes diversity collapse, curated conditions (C1--C4) should show steeper diversity decline than their random counterparts (C5--C7) across generations. If parameter capacity mediates collapse, LoRA and Full-FT conditions should diverge even under identical curation. If selection pressure drives collapse, C2 ($K{=}8$, 12.5\% retention) should collapse faster than C1 ($K{=}4$, 25\% retention).

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}

We evaluate diversity at two levels: semantic content and surface format.

\paragraph{Semantic diversity.} Our primary metric is the Vendi Score \cite{placeholder_vendi}, a kernel-based diversity measure computed over sentence embeddings from all-MiniLM-L6-v2 \cite{placeholder_minilm}. The Vendi Score equals the exponential of the Shannon entropy of the eigenvalues of the kernel matrix, with higher values indicating greater diversity. We complement this with Self-BLEU \cite{placeholder_selfbleu}, defined as the average pairwise BLEU score among all responses in the evaluation set (lower indicates more diversity), and Nearest-Neighbor Distance (NND), the average cosine distance to the nearest neighbor in embedding space (higher indicates more diversity). All semantic metrics are computed over the 200 evaluation prompts with one response per prompt per generation.

\paragraph{Format diversity.} Standard embedding-based metrics can miss surface-level homogenization because semantically distinct responses may share identical templates. We introduce two format-level indicators. Opening Entropy measures the Shannon entropy of first-10-token sequences across responses; lower values indicate convergence toward fixed opening patterns. Near-Duplicate Start Rate measures the fraction of response pairs sharing a 50-character prefix; higher values indicate template repetition. These metrics capture a distinct failure mode: models that produce varied content but wrap it in repetitive formatting.

\paragraph{Quality and training dynamics.} We track mean ArmoRM score per generation to verify that quality does not degrade, and per-generation training loss to monitor learning dynamics and detect underfitting or overfitting.

\subsection{Training Details}
\label{sec:training_details}

For LoRA configurations, we set rank to 16, scaling factor $\alpha{=}32$, and apply adapters to all linear layers. For Full-FT configurations, all parameters are updated without freezing. Both use AdamW \cite{placeholder_adamw} with cosine learning rate scheduling and train for 2 epochs per generation (3 epochs for the aggressive Full-FT setting C4 when loss has not converged). The effective batch size is 16 for LoRA (batch size 4, gradient accumulation 4) and 8 for Full-FT (batch size 1, gradient accumulation 8). All experiments run on 2$\times$ NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPUs (32GB each). The complete pipeline (7 configurations $\times$ 3 generations) requires approximately 48 GPU-hours.

